WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.420
Can you guys see him through recovery?&nbsp;
Yah I’ll take it. No, I can do it.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.040
It's common practice for doctors to scrub up&nbsp;
before performing surgery or delivering babies.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.140
But this was not always the case.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.000
In the mid-19th century, Dr. Ignaz&nbsp;
Semmelweis was the first to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.020
how to prevent doctors from spreading&nbsp;
a deadly infection to pregnant women.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.340
In 1846, he was appointed as&nbsp;
an assistant in the maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.680
ward of the renowned Vienna General Hospital.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.860
While there, he noticed something peculiar.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.520
The maternal mortality rate in the&nbsp;
ward run by doctors and medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.540
students was significantly higher&nbsp;
than in the ward run by midwives.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:43.620
Between 1841 to 1846, 4 women out of&nbsp;
100 died in the clinic run by midwives.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.960
Whereas the one handled by doctors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.400
medical students had a striking 10&nbsp;
women out of 100 lose their lives.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.780
Why would mortality rates be so&nbsp;
different between the two wards?

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.360
Semmelweiss was baffled and wrote:

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.720
“Everything was in question;&nbsp;
everything seemed inexplicable;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.360
everything was doubtful. Only the large number&nbsp;
of deaths was an unquestionable reality.”

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.000
Pregnant women assigned to&nbsp;
the ward with the doctors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.280
begged to be transferred to the other ward.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.940
They were so afraid they preferred&nbsp;
to give birth on the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.480
where they had a lower chance of&nbsp;
dying from the mysterious disease.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.020
Semmelweiss vowed to get to the&nbsp;
bottom of what was going on.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.620
Then, in March 1847, came a breakthrough.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.600
His colleague Jakob Kolletschka had&nbsp;
performed an autopsy, and a student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.880
accidentally pricked his finger with a scalpel.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.660
He died from an infection similar&nbsp;
to the one that afflicted the women.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.780
Semmelweis realized doctors started each day&nbsp;
performing autopsies on the women who had died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.600
shortly after giving birth, and then, examined&nbsp;
pregnant women without washing their hands.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.900
Whereas the midwives had&nbsp;
never handled dead bodies.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.020
He hypothesized that the doctors were transferring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.520
some kind of “cadaverous particles” from&nbsp;
the dead bodies to the pregnant women.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.840
Doctors at the time didn’t know&nbsp;
that bacteria caused infections.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.960
They believed diseases were caused by bad odors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.580
humidity, the stress of giving birth,&nbsp;
or some kind of imbalance in the body.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.360
Bacterial infections entering&nbsp;
the reproductive tract can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.940
cause severe complications during or after birth.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:24.120
An infection known as puerperal fever or&nbsp;
childbed fever can lead to intense fevers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.720
foul-smelling discharge, and excruciating pain.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.900
Death often occurred within days of giving birth.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.440
Newborns could also contract&nbsp;
the infection and die.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:40.620
So, Semmelweis ordered his medical staff to wash&nbsp;
their hands with a disinfectant chemically related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.600
to chlorine to remove the smell of decaying&nbsp;
corpses…before attending to the pregnant women.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.240
The mortality rate dropped immediately.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:55.020
In 1848, the clinic run by doctors and medical&nbsp;
students fell to 1 death per 100 births,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.680
which was slightly below the rate&nbsp;
of the clinic run by midwives.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.460
When Semmelweis wrote to the heads&nbsp;
of several maternity clinics about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.420
his discovery, you’d think they’d&nbsp;
be eager to embrace hand-washing.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.920
But they refused.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.180
The prominent American obstetrician&nbsp;
Charles Meigs famously said:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.620
“Doctors are gentlemen, and&nbsp;
gentlemen’s hands are clean.”

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.800
Many doctors pushed back&nbsp;
against the suggestion that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.380
they were to blame for their patients’ deaths.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.380
It didn’t help that Semmelweis waited 13 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.100
before officially publishing his&nbsp;
findings, in which he lamented:

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.980
“...my teachings are either ignored or attacked.”

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.600
He continued to urge the medical&nbsp;
community to listen to him, writing:

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.320
“...this truth must be made&nbsp;
known to everyone concerned.”

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.540
His book was long and included heavily&nbsp;
convoluted passages that didn’t help his case.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.720
Semmelweis was also difficult to deal with.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.640
He’s been described as having “...a temper&nbsp;
like a rattlesnake and was outrageously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.480
rude to anyone who questioned his&nbsp;
ideas,” said Dr. Irvine Loudon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.560
who was a medical historian and author&nbsp;
of "The Tragedy of Childbed Fever."

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.200
Not long after Semmelweis made his discovery,&nbsp;
the head of the obstetrics department at Vienna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.540
General Hospital refused to renew his contract,&nbsp;
another example of the resistance to his ideas.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.320
He abruptly left Vienna to&nbsp;
return home to Budapest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.340
where he worked at a hospital and&nbsp;
continued to advocate for hand washing.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.320
Despite virtually eliminating maternal&nbsp;
mortality at the hospital in Budapest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.720
his ideas were still rejected.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.940
The professor of obstetrics at the&nbsp;
University of Pest, Ede Flórián Birly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:38.340
maintained his belief that childbed fever&nbsp;
was caused by uncleanliness of the bowel.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.000
When Birly passed, Semmelweis&nbsp;
took over his position at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.180
university and grew increasingly erratic.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.040
He wrote angry letters to his critics,&nbsp;
calling them “irresponsible murderers.”

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:55.440
He became severely depressed and began&nbsp;
drinking heavily and spending more time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.640
away from his wife and five children&nbsp;
and in the company of prostitutes.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.360
The University fired him for&nbsp;
his inappropriate behavior.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.320
It’s believed he may have&nbsp;
developed early-onset dementia.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:13.140
Or perhaps, the criticism of his life’s work&nbsp;
caused him to suffer a mental breakdown.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:20.640
In 1865, Austrian physician Ferdinand Ritter&nbsp;
von Hebra lured him to a mental asylum in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.720
Vienna under the premise of showing Semmelweis the&nbsp;
facilities. Landes-Irren-Anstalt in Lazarettgasse

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.460
When Semmelweis realized he was actually being&nbsp;
admitted as a patient, he tried to leave.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:35.400
That’s when several guards beat him up, put him&nbsp;
in a straitjacket, and threw him into a cell.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.660
He died two weeks later at the age of 47.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.180
Very few people attended his funeral.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.980
His autopsy revealed he died&nbsp;
from blood poisoning from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.920
wound on his hand that he could&nbsp;
have sustained from the beating.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.760
Ironically, the man who spent&nbsp;
his life fighting against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.280
spread of infections died from an infection.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:03.540
Two years after his death, French microbiologist&nbsp;
Louis Pasteur made a monumental discovery.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.260
Pasteur realized that bacteria were spoiling wine.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:11.700
And that these microorganisms were not&nbsp;
only responsible for spoiling food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.640
drink but also for spreading infectious diseases.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:21.000
Unlike Pasteur, Semmelweis had not taken the&nbsp;
time to examine particles under a microscope.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.140
Had he been a more diligent scientist,
If he had, he would have seen that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.060
were teeming with bacteria, which could have&nbsp;
made his case for handwashing more convincing.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.660
Pasteur’s work inspired Scottish surgeon Sir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.840
Joseph Lister to introduce&nbsp;
the concept of antisepsis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.240
the practice of using chemical agents to&nbsp;
prevent the growth of bacteria on living tissue.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.600
Listerine is named after Dr. Lister.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.960
By this point, Semmelweis was all but&nbsp;
forgotten; Lister had never even heard of him!

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:58.140
Hand washing and sterilizing medical instruments&nbsp;
finally started to become standard practice in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:04.200
1860s, twenty years after Semmelweis warned&nbsp;
doctors about the importance of hygiene.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.320
Had his sanitation techniques&nbsp;
been implemented earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.800
there’s no telling how many&nbsp;
lives would have been saved.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.000
Given that the death rate for women who&nbsp;
gave birth in hospitals was as high as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.340
20% in some instances, it could have been&nbsp;
in the hundreds of thousands or even more.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.740
It was only decades later that Semmelweis&nbsp;
finally started to gain recognition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.240
as the medical community became more&nbsp;
receptive of the germ theory of disease.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.320
He’s now widely known as the “savior of mothers”.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.600
Semmelweis once said:

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.940
“When I look back upon the past, I can&nbsp;
only dispel the sadness which falls upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:47.460
me by gazing into that happy future&nbsp;
when the infection will be banished.”

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.040
Sadly, he never lived to see that day.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.920
As we've seen with Semmelweis's work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.700
the power of scientific thinking can&nbsp;
lead to life-saving advancements.

